<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Despotate of Epirus</h1><p class="st-meta">1205 CE – 1479 CE · capital Arta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine Greek successor state in north-western Greece founded after 1204, a rival of Nicaea that passed through Greek, Italian and Serbian dynasties.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-i-komnenos-doukas">Michael Komnenos Doukas</a>, a relative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelos-dynasty">Angelos</a> emperors, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> around 1205, ruling from Arta. His half-brother Theodore expanded the state, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1224 and creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/empire-of-thessalonica">Empire of Thessalonica</a>, until his defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa">Klokotnitsa</a> in 1230. The title despot was given to later rulers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers kept Byzantine administration and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church structures and claimed the imperial heritage against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>. Arta flourished, with churches such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panagia-parigoritissa">Panagia Parigoritissa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latins">Latins</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> but was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> in 1259. The Orsini of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> took over in 1318.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dushan</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in 1348. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a> reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> and Arta from 1411, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Ioannina in 1430, Arta in 1449 and the last fortresses in 1479.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/despotate-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Despotate of Epirus" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_1205-1230-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1205 CE – 1479 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Michael I Komnenos Doukas, Michael II Komnenos Doukas, Carlo I Tocco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Despotate of Arta, State of Epirus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1342&amp;focus=despotate-of-epirus" aria-label="Despotate of Epirus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5199 774 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Despotate of Epirus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5519 1030L5483 954M5519 1030L5519 1030M5519 1030L5563 982M5519 1030L5709 970M5519 1030L5521 939M5519 1030L5349 976M5519 1030L5709 970M5519 1030L5563 982"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5483 954h0M5519 1030h0M5563 982h0M5709 970h0M5521 939h0M5349 976h0M5709 970h0M5563 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5519 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5519 